%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0A8C11C8] # Mesh Analysis
%[text] [⇦ DC Circuit Analysis Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] **Learning Goals**
%[text] - Use mesh analysis to compute currents through a linear circuit.
%[text] - Solve the matrix form of circuit equations in MATLAB.
%[text] - Model and analyze linear circuits in Simscape. \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_941DFA1C] ## Overview
%[text] If a linear circuit has only one loop, it is typically a simple task to solve for the current. However, if the circuit has several branches, the current flowing through each branch is not obvious. Mesh analysis is a technique that solves this problem. You can use mesh analysis to solve for the currents flowing through a linear circuit with multiple branches. In the essential step of this technique, Kirchhoff's voltage law is applied to identify an equation for each mesh (a *mesh* is any closed loop through the circuit).
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_793E3A74] ### **Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL)**
%[text] The sum of voltages around a closed loop of a circuit (known as a mesh) is zero. Mathematically:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\sum\_{k} V\_k = 0${"altText":"\sum\_{k} V\_k = 0"}
%[text] where $V\_k${"altText":"V\_k"} is the potential difference across the $k^{th}${"altText":"k^{th}"} element of the loop. Applying Ohm's law gives the potential drop across each resistor: $V = IR${"altText":"V = IR"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![kvl.png](text:image:44a7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The potential drop across the resistors in this closed loop must equal the potential generated by the voltage source:* $-V\_1 + I\_1 R\_1 + I\_1 R\_2 + I\_1 R\_3 = 0${"altText":"-V\_1 + I\_1 R\_1 + I\_1 R\_2 + I\_1 R\_3 = 0"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_572EAC45] ### **Steps**
%[text] There are three major steps in mesh analysis:
%[text] 1. Define a current for each essential mesh.
%[text] 2. Write an equation for each essential mesh by applying KVL.
%[text] 3. Set up a matrix equation for the currents and solve. \
%[text] In the first step, you define a current variable for each essential mesh. An *essential mesh* is a circuit loop that does not contain other loops.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![twomesh.png](text:image:5a12)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Example circuit. The two interior meshes (A) and (B) are essential. The outer mesh (C) contains the inner meshes and is therefore unessential.*
%[text] With the meshes identified, you can define a current variable for each mesh – for example, $I\_A${"altText":"I\_A"} and $I\_B${"altText":"I\_B"}. Note that, while you must assign a direction for the current, the direction is arbitrary. As long as you use the variable consistently, the selected direction is unimportant.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![twomesh\_labels.png](text:image:5d4f)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Example circuit with mesh currents* $I\_A${"altText":"I\_A"} and $I\_B${"altText":"I\_B"}.
%[text] Applying KVL to mesh (A) yields 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $-V\_1 + I\_A R\_1 + (I\_A + I\_B)\\cdot R\_3 + I\_A R\_4 = 0${"altText":"-V\_1 + I\_A R\_1 + (I\_A + I\_B)\cdot R\_3 + I\_A R\_4 = 0"}
%[text] **Note:** The actual current flowing through a circuit element may equal a sum of several mesh currents. In this case, the current flowing through $R\_3${"altText":"R\_3"} is the sum $I\_A + I\_B${"altText":"I\_A + I\_B"}. Mesh currents are logical partitions and can be easily confused with branch currents.
%[text] The derived equation has two unknowns: $I\_A${"altText":"I\_A"} and $I\_B${"altText":"I\_B"}. To be solved, an additional equation is needed. Applying KVL again to the second essential mesh will yield the necessary equation. To solve the resulting system, you can define a matrix system. Matrices provide a natural framework: they can concisely capture the many linear equations that model the circuit network. In the following example, the matrix form is derived and used to solve for the mesh currents.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ACA535AC] ## **Example** 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_57A52399] Use mesh analysis to solve for the currents through the circuit with the given values.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![circuit1.png](text:image:1902)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Circuit diagram*
%[text]  $\\begin{array}{l}\nR\_1 = 10 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_2 = 20 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_3 = 15 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_4 = 2 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_5 =  330 \\ \\Omega\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{l} R\_1 = 10 \ \Omega \\ R\_2 = 20 \ \Omega \\ R\_3 = 15 \ \Omega \\ R\_4 = 2 \ \Omega \\ R\_5 =  330 \ \Omega \end{array}"} $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_1 = 1.5 \\ \\text{V}\n\\\\\nV\_2 = 6 \\ \\text{V}\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{l} V\_1 = 1.5 \ \text{V} \\ V\_2 = 6 \ \text{V} \end{array}"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3AFF3E45] ### **Step 1.** **Define the essential mesh currents.**
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![circuit1\_loops.png](text:image:52b9)
%[text] The current in each essential mesh is assigned a variable. In locations where the loops overlap, the current through the branch is the sum or difference of the mesh currents. The arbitrarily defined directions determine the signs of the currents.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AF801705] ### **Step 2. A**pply KVL.
%[text] **Mesh (1).** The first loop has four terms, one for each circuit element. By Kirchhoff's voltage law, the sum of voltages around the closed loop must be zero:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $-V\_1 + R\_1\\cdot(I\_1 -I\_2) + R\_4\\cdot( I\_1 + I\_3) + R\_5 I\_1 = 0${"altText":"-V\_1 + R\_1\cdot(I\_1 -I\_2) + R\_4\cdot( I\_1 + I\_3) + R\_5 I\_1 = 0"}
%[text] $I\_1${"altText":"I\_1"} and $I\_2${"altText":"I\_2"} were defined in opposing directions, so their difference is taken. $I\_1${"altText":"I\_1"} and $I\_3${"altText":"I\_3"}, on the other hand, both travel in the same direction through $R\_4${"altText":"R\_4"}, so their sum is used.
%[text] **Mesh (2).** The second loop has three terms.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $R\_1\\cdot(I\_2 - I\_1) + R\_2 I\_2 + R\_3\\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) = 0${"altText":"R\_1\cdot(I\_2 - I\_1) + R\_2 I\_2 + R\_3\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) = 0"}
%[text] **Mesh (3).** The third loop also has three terms.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $-V\_2 + R\_3\\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) + R\_4\\cdot(I\_3 + I\_1) = 0${"altText":"-V\_2 + R\_3\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) + R\_4\cdot(I\_3 + I\_1) = 0"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_85C694D9] ### **Step 3.** Set up the matrix equation and solve.
%[text] **Set up the matrix equation.**
%[text] Before setting up the matrix equation, rewrite the equations in terms of the unknown $I\_i${"altText":"I\_i"}'s and move the constant terms to the right-hand side.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rlcrl}\n-V\_1 + R\_1\\cdot(I\_1 -I\_2) + R\_4\\cdot( I\_1 + I\_3) + R\_5 I\_1 &= 0 &\\rightarrow &(R\_1 + R\_4 + R\_5) \\cdot I\_1 - R\_1 I\_2 + R\_4 I\_3 &= V\_1\n\\\\\nR\_1\\cdot(I\_2 - I\_1) + R\_2 I\_2 + R\_3\\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) &= 0 &\\rightarrow &-R\_1 I\_1 + (R\_1 + R\_2 + R\_3)\\cdot I\_2 + R\_3 I\_3 &= 0\n\\\\\n-V\_2 + R\_3\\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) + R\_4\\cdot(I\_3 + I\_1) &= 0 &\\rightarrow & R\_4 I\_1 + R\_3 I\_2 + (R\_3 + R\_4) \\cdot I\_3 &= V\_2\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{rlcrl} -V\_1 + R\_1\cdot(I\_1 -I\_2) + R\_4\cdot( I\_1 + I\_3) + R\_5 I\_1 &= 0 &\rightarrow &(R\_1 + R\_4 + R\_5) \cdot I\_1 - R\_1 I\_2 + R\_4 I\_3 &= V\_1 \\ R\_1\cdot(I\_2 - I\_1) + R\_2 I\_2 + R\_3\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) &= 0 &\rightarrow &-R\_1 I\_1 + (R\_1 + R\_2 + R\_3)\cdot I\_2 + R\_3 I\_3 &= 0 \\ -V\_2 + R\_3\cdot(I\_2 + I\_3) + R\_4\cdot(I\_3 + I\_1) &= 0 &\rightarrow & R\_4 I\_1 + R\_3 I\_2 + (R\_3 + R\_4) \cdot I\_3 &= V\_2 \end{array}"} 
%[text] Define the vector of unknown currents as
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{I} = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\\\ I\_2 \\\\ I\_3 \\end{array} \\right\]${"altText":"\mathbf{I} = \left\[ \begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\ I\_2 \\ I\_3 \end{array} \right\]"} 
%[text] Then, the system is converted into matrix notation $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"\mathbf{A} \mathbf{I} = \mathbf{b}"}, using the coefficients of the unknown $I\_i${"altText":"I\_i"}'s to build the coefficient matrix.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\left( \\begin{array}{rl}  \nR\_1 + R\_4 + R\_5  & \\ \\ -R\_1 &  \\ \\ R\_4\\\\\n-R\_1 &\\ \\  R\_1 + R\_2 + R\_3 &  \\ \\ R\_3 \\\\\nR\_4 & \\ \\ R\_3 &  \\ \\ R\_3 + R\_4 \\\\\n\\end{array} \\right)\n\\left( \\begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\\\ I\_2 \\\\ I\_3 \\end{array} \\right)\n=\n\\left( \\begin{array}{l} V\_1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ V\_2 \\end{array} \\right)${"altText":"\left( \begin{array}{rl}   R\_1 + R\_4 + R\_5  & \ \ -R\_1 &  \ \ R\_4\\ -R\_1 &\ \  R\_1 + R\_2 + R\_3 &  \ \ R\_3 \\ R\_4 & \ \ R\_3 &  \ \ R\_3 + R\_4 \\ \end{array} \right) \left( \begin{array}{l} I\_1 \\ I\_2 \\ I\_3 \end{array} \right) = \left( \begin{array}{l} V\_1 \\ 0 \\ V\_2 \end{array} \right)"}
%[text] **Solve the matrix equation in MATLAB.**
%[text] To solve the matrix system in MATLAB, start by defining the values of the resistors and voltage sources. 
% Given values
R1 = 10;
R2 = 20;
R3 = 15;
R4 = 2;
R5 = 330;
V1 = 1.5;
V2 = 6;
%[text] Next, define the coefficient matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"\mathbf{A}"} and constant vector $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"\mathbf{b}"}.
% Matrix system
A = [R1+R4+R5,      -R1,     R4; 
          -R1, R1+R2+R3,     R3;
           R4,       R3,  R3+R4];
b = [V1; 0; V2]
%[text] Then, solve the matrix system with the backslash (`\`) operator. To solve $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"\mathbf{A} \mathbf{I} = \mathbf{b}"}, use
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] I = A\b
%[text] ```
% Solution for the mesh currents
I = A\b
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BC84B268] ## **Simscape model analysis**
%[text] You can check your result by creating a model of the electrical circuit in Simscape and then measuring the currents. For this example, a model of the circuit has already been created. A virtual multimeter is included for taking measurements in the circuit. 
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:6eb1) **Activity.** Perform the following steps to validate the result of the mesh analysis.
%[text] 1\. Open the example model:
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![lab\_model.png](text:image:92c7)](<matlab: MeshAnalysisExample>)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  [`MeshAnalysisExample.slx`](<matlab: MeshAnalysisExample>)
%[text] **Note:** Watch [this 2-minute instructional video](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/81383420/145589989-f7ae4a32-cab3-405d-b7e8-476e34d697b5.mp4) to learn how to use the virtual multimeter.
%[text] 2\. In the next few steps, you will measure the third mesh current $I\_3${"altText":"I\_3"} using the multimeter. To measure current, you must connect the multimeter in series with the branch. Start by deleting the existing circuit branch through which only $I\_3${"altText":"I\_3"} flows. Left-click the branch and press the delete key.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![g1683.png](text:image:31da)
%[text] 3\. Drag the lead connections (at the bottom of the multimeter) to the circuit. Ensure that the leads are connected so that $I\_3${"altText":"I\_3"} flows in the correct direction through the multimeter. By convention, current flows from positive (red lead) to negative (black lead). Connect the multimeter so that $I\_3${"altText":"I\_3"} flows into the red lead and out of the black lead.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![image1563.png](text:image:0b5d)
%[text] 4\. Run the model and then set the dial to measure mA⎓. If correct, there should be 501 mA flowing through this branch (since $I\_3 = 0.501${"altText":"I\_3 = 0.501"} A). If the value is not 501 mA, verify that you have connected the leads to the correct nodes and are measuring mA⎓. After taking your measurement, stop the model.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![image1626.png](text:image:3b1a)
%[text] 5\. Repeat steps 2-4 to measure the mesh currents $I\_1${"altText":"I\_1"} and $I\_2${"altText":"I\_2"}. If correct, you should find $I\_1 = 3.46${"altText":"I\_1 = 3.46"} mA and $I\_2 = 168${"altText":"I\_2 = 168"} mA, the currents calculated in the mesh analysis.
%[text] 
